Aṅguttara I–III: distinctions worth retaining (@buddhist-an-ones-to-threes: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-numerical-discourses, #philosophy @buddhist-an-fours-to-sevens, #buddhism @buddhist-an-fours-to-sevens) { r_6F270NS2RXQMDPF4RSMFZ367MV
**Read:** the books of ones, twos, and threes in [The Numerical Discourses of the Buddha](../sources/Numerical-discourses.lingua#r_16JWQQQAWBJN7CX05AEG4WF5FR), in sequence. Complete these books rather than only reading the examples selected for a note.

A numbered teaching selects distinctions for a purpose. Ask what its items distinguish and what would be lost if two items were collapsed. Relate a list to a passage in another collection instead of interpreting it only through its numerical shape.

**Consolidate:** Choose one list, supply its passage and context, and explain its practical or doctrinal use. Compare another presentation without forcing a one-to-one correspondence.

**Check:** show how one distinction clarifies a difficulty retained from the Saṃyutta, Majjhima, or Suttanipāta. Keep AN numbers with your examples.

**Continue:** [Aṅguttara IV–VII: qualities, conditions, and gradations](AN-Fours-to-sevens.lingua#r_5ZG3GR9SQM6PSV8PBH02RFC6J7).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
